  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-214026",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we reduce our congruence to a congruence of the form $ax≡b,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x-22 & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ 9x-22+22 & ≡7+22\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ 9x & ≡29\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ 9x & ≡2\\cdot 11+7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ 9x & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=9$ and $n=11$, and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(9,11)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11).$ To find the inverse of $9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)$, we compute the exponents of $9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)$ until we get a result of $1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{2} & ≡81≡7\\cdot 11+4≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {9}^{3} & ≡4\\cdot 9≡36≡3\\cdot 11+3≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {9}^{4} & ≡3\\cdot 9≡27≡2\\cdot 11+5≡5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {9}^{5} & ≡5\\cdot 9≡45≡4\\cdot 11+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $5\\cdot 9≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11),$ which means that $5$ is the multiplicative inverse of $9$ (modulo $11$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $5,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x & ≡7 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ 5\\cdot 9x & ≡5\\cdot 7 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ (5\\cdot 9)x & ≡35 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡2+3\\cdot 11 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ x & ≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11).$",
